To achieve the desired effect, you can use CSS transitions and the `:hover` pseudo-class. Here's an example CSS rule that applies the dynamic shadow effect and rotation:

```css
div {
  position: relative;
  width: 200px;
  height: 200px;
  background-color: #ccc;
  transition: box-shadow 0.3s, transform 0.3s;
}

div:hover {
  box-shadow: 0 0 10px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
  transform: rotateY(360deg);
}

div:hover::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 1) 100%);
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.3s;
}

div:hover::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 1) 100%);
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.3s;
}

div:hover::before, div:hover::after {
  filter: blur(100px);
  z-index: -1;
}

div:hover::before {
  opacity: 1;
}

div:hover::after {
  opacity: 0.5;
}
```

In this example, the `div` element is initially styled with a gray background color. When the user hovers over the `div`, the `box-shadow` property is transitioned from no shadow to a shadow with a blur radius of 10px and a spread radius of 10px, resulting in a total blur radius of 20px. Additionally, the `transform` property is transitioned to rotate the `div` around the Y-axis from 0 degrees to 360 degrees.

To create the smooth transition effect for the shadow color, two pseudo-elements (`::before` and `::after`) are used. These pseudo-elements have a gradient background that transitions from transparent to white, which is then blurred using the `filter` property. The `opacity` property is used to control the fade-in effect of the shadow.

Please note that this CSS rule assumes that the `div` element has a `class` or `id` assigned to it. You can modify the selector as needed to target your specific `div` element.

