function getDivisibleElements(arr, num) {
  const divisibleSet = new Set();

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] % num === 0) {
      divisibleSet.add(arr[i]);
    }
  }

  return Array.from(divisibleSet);
}
```

Explanation:
- We start by creating a new `Set` called `divisibleSet` to store the unique elements that are divisible by `num`.
- We iterate through each element of the input array using a `for` loop.
- Inside the loop, we check if the current element is divisible by `num` using the modulo operator (`%`). If it is divisible, we add it to the `divisibleSet` using the `add()` method.
- Finally, we convert the `Set` to an array using `Array.from()` and return the resulting array.

The time complexity of this function is O(n) because we iterate through each element of the input array once. The space complexity is O(1) because the size of the `divisibleSet` does not depend on the size of the input array.

